WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.640
So what I did in my book The Value of
Everything was to bring it down

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.469
to first principles and ask: could it be
that also we've just lost our

00:00:10.469 --> 00:00:14.519
way and stopped really debating
something that actually in the course of

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:18.510
the last two hundred years of
thinking about the economy was really

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:24.300
central? This question of: what
is value? What is value creation? What is

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.429
value extraction? And what happens when we either by mistake or on purpose

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:34.680
end up rewarding value extraction more
than value creation? Do we get something

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.370
called 'value destruction'? But I
just keep using that word 'value' there,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:44.100
but what is value? And notice, by the way,
that the subtitle is not 'Makers vs.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.570
Takers,' because that's really static. That
would lead to the rest of my forty

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.140
minutes here kind of making a list of
big bad finance, hedge funds, credit

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.440
default swaps—bad. And then saying that something else is good.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.699
That's not what I'm going to do. 'Making and Taking.' The reason I chose

00:00:59.699 --> 00:01:05.700
that kind of verb is that we can change
things. We can—and I hope perhaps we can

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.670
talk about it also in the Q&amp;A—we can
fundamentally reform how finance is

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.450
operating and how certain business
practices have kind of gone astray and

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:21.180
how government has just become a bit too lame. But what's really interesting is

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.710
that this kind of concept of making and
taking often comes back after

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:30.299
every financial crisis. So I pulled out
this quote here, which is a very nice one

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:35.460
by Big Bill Haywood, the first industrial
trade unionist in the US, writing at the

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.140
time of the Great Crash in 1929. And it's
really interesting because you just

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.649
replace the word 'gold barons' by
'financial sector' and it kind of rings

00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:47.790
what a lot of people and communities
around the world were saying. So: "The

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.119
barbarous gold barons, they didn't find
the gold, they didn't mine the gold, they

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.170
didn't mill the gold, but by some weird
magic (alchemy), all the gold belongs to

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.909
them." And so this is the kind of making
and taking. And you know this is kind of

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:04.770
the accusation. But so what does this
mean? What does it mean to actually think

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.090
about the economy in terms of certain
areas being productive, certain

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.150
being unproductive, and really thinking
about how to make sure we're steering

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.650
activities inside what I call in the book the

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.750
'production boundary.' And unfortunately
what happens when we no longer talk

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:25.390
about value and just kind of assume Silicon Valley's full of wealth

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:31.480
creators, we end up allowing stories,
literally just stories and narratives

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.710
and discourses about value and wealth
creation to take over. And what I'm going

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:41.830
to basically argue is that when that
happens, we allow value extraction to be

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.740
disguised as value creation. And you know, it's not that the landlords in the

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.160
1700's—and I'll get to them in a minute—weren't extracting value; but they didn't

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.570
pretend to be innovative, didn't pretend
to be value creators, they just said, "Give

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:59.800
me your damn money," and people gave them the money. Adam Smith called landlords

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.480
thieves. There was no masquerading. And again I'm

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.800
not gonna—don't worry, I'm sure there's
many tech people here in the room—I'm

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.740
not gonna start making accusations of,
again, whether it's tech or finance or

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:20.890
government officials as value extractors.
But this kind of masquerading exercise

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:27.970
is a problem. And you know, there's—I was really struck how one year, just one year

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:32.920
after the crisis, the head of Goldman
Sachs Lloyd Blankfein, said with a

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.910
completely straight face—it was not an
after-dinner speech where he's trying to

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:40.750
make people laugh—completely
serious, look it up just google it. And

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:47.500
the FT put this quote that Goldman Sachs workers were the most productive in the

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:53.440
world. I mean, this is a period
where they had just been bailed out ten

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.820
billion dollars by the US government
because of what happened with the

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.390
financial crisis. There was plenty of
evidence that it was in fact to a large

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:05.380
extent not only the activities of the
large investment banks that at least

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.370
partially led to the financial crisis,
and yet this bold statement of we are

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:13.239
the most productive in the world. And so
this whole concept of how do we measure

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:18.010
productivity: do teachers say we are the
most productive in the world? And if not,

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:22.210
could it be that how we are actually
accounting for value in the system

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:26.169
creates actually a self-fulfilling
prophecy? That it allows some actors in

00:04:26.169 --> 00:04:29.669
the economy to be very
bold and arrogant and and to be able

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:34.259
to claim—even just logically in terms of
how we do the counting—that they are so

00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:37.919
productive? But then again I mentioned
story, so this idea that again wealth

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:43.710
creators in Silicon Valley or in the
case of Brexit—Brexit, I'm still

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:48.030
hoping it doesn't happen—happened, this idea that we have to at

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:53.250
least protect those really valuable
sectors which which were producing value

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.960
for the UK economy, so financial services. And I'll mention also later the way that

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:02.130
the word 'value' is used in sectors like
the pharmaceutical industry to justify

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:07.259
very high increases and prices. So,
recently a price of an antibiotic went

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:12.030
up by four hundred percent overnight. And the idea that value-based pricing could

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:17.039
somehow be used to justify that is quite
extraordinary. And on the opposite

00:05:17.039 --> 00:05:19.860
spectrum, I've read—I mentioned teachers. You don't hear teachers calling

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:24.150
themselves, 'We are so productive,' but also the state itself. How we talk about the

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:28.169
state, we don't talk about it really as
creating value. At best we talk about it

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:33.930
as redistributing value or enabling or
de-risking the value creators. So these

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:37.349
are really just words and narratives and
discourses which are kind of just—I

00:05:37.349 --> 00:05:40.680
don't wanna say bullshit—but just kind of
stories, right? Economics is not nuclear

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.060
physics. And we've allowed, by not
actually talking about value, these

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.400
stories to be out there and almost not
even questioned.

